9-11-2025 REST API Design with Java


Why REST matters
    - enables systems to communicate over standard HTTP
    - interoperability between different platforms and languages
    - statelessness simplifies server design and improves scalability
    - evolvability, can evolve with versioning while maintaining backward compatibility

CRUD operations
    - create - POST
    - read - GET
    - update - PUT/PATCH
    - delete - DELETE

API
    - a published contract that lets one piece of software use another's functionality
        - can use without seeing internals, promise about behaviour, not implementation
    - producer and consumer
    - Many styles: REST, SOAP, gRPC, GraphQL, etc.

REST
    - Representational State Transfer
    - architectural style, set of constraints not protocol or standard
    - stateless, every request must carry all the context the server needs, nothing is remembered between calls
    - resources as representations, clients exchange representations (JSON) of server-side resources instead of manipulating them directly
    - uses HTTP deliberately, instead of layering a new protocol on top of it
        - no REST RFC (request for comments), only constraints to satisfy
    - Maturity Levels
        - Level 0-3, Level 2 general usage in the world, Level 3 is hypermedia driven, not widely used


REST Constraints (Slide 14)
    - Client-Server
    - Stateless
    - Cacheable
    - Uniform Interface
    - Layered System
    - Code on Demand (optional)

Client-Server Architecture
    - Separation of concerns
    - Independent evolution
    - Portability (same API can server different clients)

Cacheability (Slide 21)
    - Tell clients and proxies how long a representation stays valid
    - GET request can be cached until the record changes, cutting load on service
    - explicit, not assumed, uncacheable resources must be marked as such

Uniform Interface (SLide 23)
    - REST defines a uniform way for clients to interact with resources.

Layered System Architecture
    - Separation of concerns, each layer has a specific role and interacts only with adjacent layers

Resource Based API Design
    - Resources, key business entities or concepts
    - Each resource is identified by a unique URI, and is manipulated using standard HTTP methods

Identifying API Resources
    - Resources, nouns or business entities that your API exposes

HTTP Request Structure
    - Request Line
        - Method with URI
    - Headers
        - Auth
        - Content-Type
        - Accept
    - Body (optional)
        - POST, PUT, PATCH carry a body
        - GET and DELETE normally do not

    - Header, blank line, request body

HTTP Response Structure
    - Status Line
        - HTTP version, status code, reason phrase
    - Headers
        - Metadata about the response
    - Body
        - Contains actual data returned by the server

HTTP Methods
    - GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE
        - PUT, Replace an entire resource
        - PATCH, partial update
    - Only GET is safe
    - Idempotent
        - operations that can be performed multiple time with the same effect

HTTP Status Codes
    - 1xx, Informational
    - 2xx, Success
        - 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content
    - 3xx, Redirection (further action is needed to complete the request)
        - 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified
    - 4xx, Client Error
        - 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 403 Forbidden, 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict
    - 5xx, Server Error
        - 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable

Request and Response Samples (Slide 49)
    - camelCase for field names
    - nesting reflects structures

Content-Type and Accept Headers
    - describes what was sent, accept describes what the client is willing to receive
    - header that describes the media type of the resource, e.g., application/json

API Error response design
    - One consistent JSON error shape lets every client handle every failure the same way
    - Fields
        - timestamp, status, error, message, path, traceId

Pagination, filtering and sorting
    - Pagination, breaks a large dataset into smaller chunks (Pages) so clients can load data efficiently
    - Filtering, allows clients to request only the data that matches specific conditions, reducing payload size and improving performance
        - eg GET /api/products?category=electronics&price<1000
    - Sorting, allows clients to specify the order in which results are returned based on one or more fields
        - eg GET /api/products?sort=price,asc&sort=name,desc
        - always does the first sort first, then the second

API Versioning
    - a breaking chance needs a way to reach only the clients that are ready for it
    - allows you to evolve your API over time without breaking existing clients
    - URI versioning
        - most common in the wild
    - Header-Based Versioning
        - better for internal APIs, clean URIs
    - Query Parameter Versioning

OpenAPI

Request and Response Schemas
    - define the structure, data types, and validation rules for the data sent to your API

API-First vs Code-First
    - API-First
        - Contract First, generate stubs, then implement
    - Code-First
        - Write Spring controllers first, then generate OpenAPI from annotations







